######## Section name and subsequence ########
 part 28 of MobyDick: sperm whale, greenland whale


 :: sperm whale, greenland whale, _sequence_ ::
#######################################

 '"Unfitness to pursue our research in the unfathomable waters." "Impenetrable veil covering our knowledge of the cetacea." "A field strewn with thorns." "All these incomplete indications but serve to torture us naturalists."' [MobyDick] 
  " (itintab) part 28 of MobyDick: sperm whale, greenland whale 

 Nevertheless, though of real knowledge there be little, yet of books there are a plenty; and so in some small degree, with cetology, or the science of whales. [MobyDick] 
  " (itintab) part 28 of MobyDick: sperm whale, greenland whale 

 Many are the men, small and great, old and new, landsmen and seamen, who have at large or in little, written of the whale. [MobyDick] 
  " (itintab) part 28 of MobyDick: sperm whale, greenland whale 

 Of the names in this list of whale authors, only those following Owen ever saw living whales; and but one of them was a real professional harpooneer and whaleman. [MobyDick] 
  " (itintab) part 28 of MobyDick: sperm whale, greenland whale 

 But Scoresby knew nothing and says nothing of the great sperm whale, compared with which the Greenland whale is almost unworthy mentioning. [MobyDick] 
  " (itintab) part 28 of MobyDick: sperm whale, greenland whale 

 Yet, owing to the long priority of his claims, and the profound ignorance which, till some seventy years back, invested the then fabulous or utterly unknown sperm-whale, and which ignorance to this present day still reigns in all but some few scientific retreats and whale-ports; this usurpation has been every way complete. [MobyDick] 
  " (itintab) part 28 of MobyDick: sperm whale, greenland whale 

 Reference to nearly all the leviathanic allusions in the great poets of past days, will satisfy you that the Greenland whale, without one rival, was to them the monarch of the seas. [MobyDick] 
  " (itintab) part 28 of MobyDick: sperm whale, greenland whale 

  good people all,—the Greenland whale is deposed,—the great sperm whale now reigneth! [MobyDick] 
  " (itintab) part 28 of MobyDick: sperm whale, greenland whale 

 There are only two books in being which at all pretend to put the living sperm whale before you, and at the same time, in the remotest degree succeed in the attempt. [MobyDick] 
  " (itintab) part 28 of MobyDick: sperm whale, greenland whale 

 Those books are Beale's and Bennett's; both in their time surgeons to English South-Sea whale-ships, and both exact and reliable men. [MobyDick] 
  " (itintab) part 28 of MobyDick: sperm whale, greenland whale 

 The original matter touching the sperm whale to be found in their volumes is necessarily small; but so far as it goes, it is of excellent quality, though mostly confined to scientific description. [MobyDick] 
  " (itintab) part 28 of MobyDick: sperm whale, greenland whale 

 Now the various species of whales need some sort of popular comprehensive classification, if only an easy outline one for the present, hereafter to be filled in all its departments by subsequent laborers. [MobyDick] 
  " (itintab) part 28 of MobyDick: sperm whale, greenland whale 

 I promise nothing complete; because any human thing supposed to be complete, must for that very reason infallibly be faulty. [MobyDick] 
  " (itintab) part 28 of MobyDick: sperm whale, greenland whale 

 To grope down into the bottom of the sea after them; to have one's hands among the unspeakable foundations, ribs, and very pelvis of the world; this is a fearful thing. [MobyDick] 
  " (itintab) part 28 of MobyDick: sperm whale, greenland whale 

